# rsess config — copy to ~/.rsess/config (sourced as POSIX sh).
# Everything here is OPTIONAL; sane defaults apply if the file is absent.

# --- rsess preferences -------------------------------------------------------
# RSESS_SOCKET=rsess           # remote `tmux -L <socket>` name (isolates rsess
#                              # sessions from any admin/user tmux on the host)
# RSESS_TIMEOUT=120            # seconds `run` waits before reporting a timeout
# RSESS_REMOTE_DIR=.rsess      # logs/run files dir under the remote $HOME
# RSESS_REMOTE_BIN=.bin/tmux   # where --upload-tmux puts the bundled binary
# RSESS_MIN_TMUX=1.8           # minimum acceptable system tmux version
# RSESS_CONTROL_PERSIST=60s    # ssh ControlMaster keep-alive after last use

# --- optional target aliases -------------------------------------------------
# A convenience layer over ssh: `rsess open <topic> <alias>` expands the alias
# to an ssh destination. Prefer putting real connection details in ~/.ssh/config
# (HostName/User/Port/ProxyJump/IdentityFile) and just naming the Host here.
#
#   rsess_alias_web="alice@203.0.113.10"
#   rsess_alias_db="behind-jump"          # a ~/.ssh/config Host with ProxyJump

# --- example: reaching hosts via a jump / reverse tunnel ---------------------
# rsess itself needs NO special code for jumps — express them in ~/.ssh/config.
# For a reverse-tunnel setup where a bastion forwards each host's sshd to a
# loopback port on the bastion, a ~/.ssh/config entry looks like:
#
#   Host site-a
#       HostName 127.0.0.1
#       Port 2222
#       User myuser
#       ProxyJump jumpuser@bastion.example.com
#       StrictHostKeyChecking no
#       UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null
#
# then:  rsess open site-a-debug site-a
#
# A second hop (host reached *through* another tunneled host) is just a
# comma-separated ProxyJump:
#       ProxyJump jumpuser@bastion.example.com,root@127.0.0.1:2200
#
# --- parametrized targets (random/temporary ports or IPs) --------------------
# When the port or IP changes every time, use a WILDCARD Host pattern whose
# ProxyCommand slices the variable out of the alias name — written once, it
# matches every value. e.g. `rsess open <topic> t-<port>` for any <port>:
#
#   Host t-*
#       User myuser
#       StrictHostKeyChecking no
#       UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null
#       ProxyCommand sh -c 'exec ssh -W 127.0.0.1:"${1#t-}" jumpuser@bastion.example.com' _ %h
